# Cursor Rules Format

<introduction>
Cursor rules serve as project-specific knowledge repositories that provide context and guidance for AI interactions. This document outlines the standard structure and formatting conventions for creating effective rules that optimize LLM comprehension and utilization.
</introduction>

<template>
```mdc
---
description: Informative description of rule's purpose and application context
globs: .cursor/rules/**/*.mdc
alwaysApply: {true or false}
---

# Rule Title

<context>
- Application scenarios for this rule
- Relevant system components or processes
</context>

<specifications>
- Key information about components, architecture, or processes
- Reference links to [[important files]] or concepts
- Documentation of patterns and conventions
</specifications>

<examples>
<example>
Effective implementation with explanation
</example>

<example type="alternative">
Alternative approach with explanation
</example>
</examples>

<critical_points>
- ALWAYS include most critical guidance first with capitalization for emphasis
- Include secondary important points in standard case
- NEVER use critical points for non-essential information
- Reference [[related_file.ext]] when specific implementation details are needed
</critical_points>
```
</template>

<organization>
<location>
Path: `.cursor/rules/`
Extension: `.mdc`
</location>

<naming_conventions>
PREFIX-name.mdc where PREFIX categorizes rules:
- 0XX: Core standards and documentation
- 1XX: Tool configuration and usage
- 3XX: Testing standards and patterns
- 1XXX: Language-specific patterns
- 2XXX: Framework-specific knowledge
- 8XX: Workflow documentation
- 9XX: Template references
- _name.mdc: Private rules
</naming_conventions>

<glob_patterns>
Common pattern examples for different rule applications:
- Core standards: .cursor/rules/*.mdc
- Language rules: src/**/*.{js,ts}
- Testing standards: **/*.test.{js,ts}
- React components: src/components/**/*.tsx
- Documentation: docs/**/*.md
- Configuration files: *.config.{js,json}
- Build artifacts: dist/**/*
- Multiple extensions: src/**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}
- Multiple files: dist/**/*, docs/**/*.md
</glob_patterns>
</organization>

<structure>
<frontmatter>
- description: Informative description of the rule's purpose and application
- globs: Pattern for files where this rule applies
- alwaysApply: {true or false} - attaches rule to every conversation when true
</frontmatter>

<content_sections>
- context: Scenarios where the rule applies
- specifications: Core knowledge, patterns, and references
- examples: Effective implementations and alternatives
- critical_points: Essential aspects of the rule
</content_sections>
</structure>

<formatting>
- Use concise Markdown as the primary format
- Employ XML section tags for better organization:
  - <context>, <template>, <specifications>, <examples>, <critical_points>
  - <example>, <danger>, <required>, <warning>
- Indent content within XML tags by 2 spaces
- Include Mermaid diagrams when they clarify concepts
- Link to relevant code files using [[filename]] syntax
- Use CAPITALIZED words for selective emphasis in critical points
- Use <warning> tags for particularly important cautions
- Balance descriptive content with emphasized guidance
</formatting>

<tag_naming>
- Choose descriptive, multi-word tag names (e.g., <data_output> instead of <output>)
- Avoid single-character or very short tag names (<o>, <p>, <h>)
- Avoid tag names that may conflict with HTML or XML reserved words:
  - Problematic: <output>, <input>, <template>, <style>, <script>
  - Better alternatives: <data_output>, <user_input>, <code_template>, <styling>, <script_code>
- Prefix tags for clarity when needed (e.g., <section_introduction>, <component_details>)
- Maintain tag name length of at least 4 characters
- Use underscore_case for multi-word tag names
</tag_naming>

<emphasis_patterns>
- <critical_points> should contain a mix of emphasized and standard text
- Use CAPITALIZED words for action verbs or prohibitions (ALWAYS, NEVER, USE, AVOID)
- Keep capitalized emphasis limited to 2-4 words per point
- Combine capitalization with informative tags for maximum LLM attention
- Place most critical information in <critical_points> section
- Use <warning> tags for information that prevents significant errors
</emphasis_patterns>

<LLM_comprehension>
- XML tags create semantic boundaries that improve LLM context understanding
- Capitalized words increase attention weight in LLM processing
- Direct file references with [[filename]] syntax enable concrete implementation
- Consistent structure with mixed emphasis patterns optimizes comprehension
- Specific section tags help LLM weight and categorize information appropriately
- Limited, strategic capitalization prevents emphasis dilution
</LLM_comprehension>

<critical_points>
- ALWAYS create frontmatter descriptions under 120 characters that clearly communicate purpose
- ALWAYS use frontmatter fields according to the structured (description, globs, alwaysApply)
- Write concise, information-rich content focused on "what" rather than "how"
- INCLUDE useful code file references using [[filename]] markdown link syntax
- Structure rules as knowledge repositories rather than command lists
- Design content for BOTH human readability AND LLM comprehension
- Use standard glob patterns without quotes (e.g., *.js, src/**/*.ts)
- Keep rules under 50 lines when possible (excluding necessary examples or diagrams)
- Include both effective and alternative examples
- Use SELECTIVE CAPITALIZATION for most critical guidance in <critical_points>
- Balance informative content with strategic emphasis for optimal LLM attention
</critical_points>
